package wosh:terminal;

/// Host-side keeper of this client's IROH identity -- the pairing
/// identity, the stable name the listener remembers this client by.
///
/// Deliberately a different animal from `identity-store`. That one is
/// sign-only and non-extractable because it guards the SSH boundary.
/// This one hands raw key material back and forth, because the iroh
/// endpoint has to be CONSTRUCTED from the pair each instantiation,
/// and because the pairing layer's stakes are low by design: it gates
/// nothing but the tunnel, and SSH -- host-key gate, real
/// authentication -- is the boundary that matters. A stolen pairing
/// key lets someone knock on the listener's door; it opens nothing.
///
/// The blob is opaque to the host: the component owns the format (it
/// mirrors the listener's own persisted-identity layout). A host
/// without durable storage returns none from `load` and accepts
/// `store` into the void; the component then simply pairs fresh each
/// run, which is exactly the old behaviour.
interface pairing-store {
  /// The blob a previous `store` saved, if any survives.
  load: async func() -> result<option<list<u8>>, string>;

  /// Persist `blob`, replacing anything stored before.
  store: async func(blob: list<u8>) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Host-side keeper of this client's SSH identity: the key never
/// crosses this interface, only its public half and signatures do.
///
/// Persistence is a HOST concern, structurally: the private half must
/// stay a non-extractable WebCrypto handle, and only the host can put
/// such a handle somewhere durable -- a browser stores the CryptoKey
/// pair in IndexedDB by structured clone, which preserves
/// non-extractability (the material never becomes readable, it just
/// survives the page). The component cannot do any of that: it holds
/// only capability handles, and those die with the instance.
///
/// The surface is deliberately sign-only rather than handing back a
/// `polymorph:webcrypto` signing-key handle: no private-key handle
/// exists anywhere in the component graph, and the component needs no
/// webcrypto import of its own. Signatures are relayed as the store
/// produces them -- the store is on the host side of the trust
/// boundary (it IS the keeper of the key), and the server verifies
/// every signature against the offered public key anyway, so a store
/// that signs with a different key than it reports surfaces as a
/// server-side authentication failure.
interface identity-store {
  /// The public half of this client's SSH identity, as a raw Ed25519
  /// public key (32 bytes). Stable across calls -- and, wherever the
  /// host has durable storage, across page loads: the authorized_keys
  /// line derived from it stays valid. The host mints the pair
  /// (non-extractable, sign-only) on first use. A host without durable
  /// storage (the native e2e gate) may mint fresh per run; that is a
  /// property of the host, not a mode of this interface.
  public-key: async func() -> result<list<u8>, string>;

  /// Sign `data` -- the SSH publickey-auth signature blob (session id,
  /// user, service, algorithm, public key) -- with the held identity,
  /// returning the raw 64-byte Ed25519 signature.
  sign: async func(data: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, string>;
}

/// Host-side keeper of this client's PASSKEY SSH identity: a WebAuthn
/// credential used directly as an SSH key.
///
/// OpenSSH has taken browser-made WebAuthn assertions as publickey
/// credentials since 8.4, under the signature algorithm
/// `webauthn-sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com`. What the target
/// needs is an ordinary `authorized_keys` line and nothing else --
/// no agent, no helper, no software installed. There is one server
/// CONFIGURATION caveat, and it is the first thing to check when this
/// fails: only OpenSSH 10.3 and later put that algorithm in the
/// default `PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms` (upstream enabled it in
/// February 2026). Every release from 8.4 onward can verify the
/// signatures, but 8.4 through 10.2 refuse the offer before looking at
/// one, logging "signature algorithm ... not in
/// PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms"; the cure is one line of sshd_config:
/// `PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms +webauthn-sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com`.
/// Nothing in the listener participates either way; it stays a dumb
/// pipe.
///
/// What this buys over `identity-store`, which is not a small list:
/// the private half lives in the platform authenticator (Secure
/// Enclave, TPM, StrongBox) rather than in browser storage; every
/// signature costs a deliberate human act, so a live tab is not a
/// standing SSH login; and sshd enforces that -- it rejects a
/// signature whose user-presence flag is clear, and can be told to
/// demand user verification with `verify-required`.
///
/// What it does NOT buy, and the reason `adopt` and `assert-unknown`
/// exist: an assertion does not return the credential's public key, and
/// WebAuthn fixes the user handle at registration -- before the key
/// exists -- so there is nowhere to stash it inside the credential. A
/// passkey that syncs to a second device, or that outlives an eviction
/// of this browser's storage, therefore arrives without the one thing
/// SSH must put on the wire.
///
/// The public half is not a secret, so there are two mundane cures.
/// Carry the `authorized_keys` line (the same string already installed
/// on the target) to the other device and `adopt` it. Or, when that
/// line is not to hand -- storage evicted, and the target unreachable
/// without it -- RECOVER it from the credential itself: two assertions
/// determine the key that made them, at the cost of two touches and
/// nothing else.
///
/// This interface is WebAuthn-shaped rather than SSH-shaped on
/// purpose. The host runs ceremonies and hands back exactly what the
/// browser gave it; the client component owns every byte of SSH
/// encoding. A host that lies about the public key produces
/// signatures the server refuses -- the same failure mode as
/// `identity-store`, resolved by the server, not by trust here.
interface passkey-store {
  /// A passkey enrolled as an SSH identity.
  record passkey-identity {
    /// The credential's public key: an uncompressed NIST P-256 point,
    /// 65 bytes, `0x04 || X || Y`. ECDSA/P-256 is not a preference --
    /// it is the only curve OpenSSH's webauthn algorithm defines.
    public-key: list<u8>,
    /// The WebAuthn Relying Party ID the credential is scoped to (for
    /// a browser, the site's domain).
    ///
    /// This is not bookkeeping: it goes verbatim into the offered key
    /// blob's `application` field, because the authenticator signs
    /// over `sha256(rp-id)` and sshd reconstructs that hash from the
    /// `application` in `authorized_keys`. The identity is therefore
    /// stamped with the origin that minted it -- a clone of this site
    /// on another domain cannot use the same line, and moving the site
    /// to a new domain means a new identity.
    relying-party: string,
  }

  /// An assertion plus the credential that produced it, for a ceremony
  /// run against a credential this client cannot yet name.
  record recovery-assertion {
    /// The credential's id, as the authenticator reported it. Two
    /// recovery ceremonies must agree on this or they are describing
    /// two different passkeys.
    credential-id: list<u8>,
    /// The Relying Party ID the ceremony ran against -- the piece of
    /// the identity that never needed recovering, since it is just this
    /// site's domain. It rides along so the recovered identity is
    /// complete without the client having to know its own origin, and
    /// it is checkable: the authenticator signed over its hash.
    relying-party: string,
    assertion: assertion,
  }

  /// One WebAuthn assertion, verbatim as the ceremony produced it.
  record assertion {
    /// `authenticatorData`: the RP ID hash, the flags byte, the
    /// signature counter, and any authenticator extension outputs.
    authenticator-data: list<u8>,
    /// `clientDataJSON`, byte for byte. Do not re-serialize it: sshd
    /// checks it against a reconstructed prefix and hashes what is
    /// sent, so a single reordered or reformatted byte fails
    /// authentication.
    client-data-json: list<u8>,
    /// The credential's ECDSA signature over
    /// `authenticator-data || sha256(client-data-json)`, in the ASN.1
    /// DER form WebAuthn returns.
    signature: list<u8>,
  }

  /// The enrolled passkey identity, or `none` where the user has not
  /// enrolled one (which is the default state -- enrolment is a
  /// deliberate act, and this client authenticates perfectly well
  /// without it).
  identity: async func() -> result<option<passkey-identity>, string>;

  /// Run a WebAuthn registration ceremony and keep the result as this
  /// client's passkey identity, replacing any previous one. The user
  /// interacts with their authenticator; a refusal or a timeout is an
  /// ordinary `err`, not a fault.
  enroll: async func() -> result<passkey-identity, string>;

  /// Adopt an identity enrolled elsewhere -- the second-device path.
  /// The credential itself is expected to be reachable from this
  /// device already (a synced passkey); what is missing, and what this
  /// supplies, is the public half. Hosts should confirm the claim
  /// before storing it, by asserting once and checking the signature
  /// against `public-key`, so that adopting the wrong passkey fails
  /// here rather than as an opaque server-side rejection later.
  adopt: async func(identity: passkey-identity) -> result<_, string>;

  /// Store an identity whose public half has already been PROVED, along
  /// with the credential it belongs to.
  ///
  /// This is `adopt` without the confirming ceremony, and exists for
  /// the recovery path, where the proof is the two assertions recovery
  /// already spent the user's patience on -- asking for a third touch
  /// to re-establish what is known would be theatre.
  ///
  /// The store is trusted here in the same narrow way it is trusted
  /// everywhere else in this design: nothing it keeps is a secret, and
  /// a wrong public key costs one failed authentication and a re-run,
  /// because the server checks the signature against the key that was
  /// offered.
  remember: async func(identity: passkey-identity, credential-id: list<u8>) -> result<_, string>;

  /// Forget the enrolled identity. The credential survives in the
  /// authenticator (only its owner can delete it there); this client
  /// simply stops offering it.
  forget: async func() -> result<_, string>;

  /// Run a WebAuthn assertion ceremony over `challenge`.
  ///
  /// `challenge` is the SSH signature blob VERBATIM, not a hash of it:
  /// sshd rebuilds the expected `clientDataJSON` prefix around the
  /// base64url of these exact bytes. The browser does that encoding
  /// itself, so pass them through untouched.
  ///
  /// Request no authenticator extensions here. Any extension output
  /// sets a flag that changes the shape of what was signed, and the
  /// simplest way to keep the wire format predictable is to ask for
  /// nothing.
  assert: async func(challenge: list<u8>) -> result<assertion, string>;

  /// Run an assertion ceremony without knowing which credential will
  /// answer, and report which one did.
  ///
  /// This is the ceremony `recover-passkey` runs, and the only one that
  /// works when nothing is enrolled: `assert` needs a stored record to
  /// name the credential and to check the result against, and recovery
  /// is precisely the situation where no such record survives. The user
  /// chooses from whatever passkeys this site has -- which is why
  /// `credential-id` comes back, so the caller can insist that two
  /// ceremonies answered from the SAME credential rather than
  /// intersecting assertions that were never going to agree.
  ///
  /// Same rules as `assert`: no extensions, and the challenge is passed
  /// through untouched.
  assert-unknown: async func(challenge: list<u8>) -> result<recovery-assertion, string>;
}

/// The browser-facing SSH terminal surface: what the static site drives
/// (through deltic) to run an interactive shell over the iroh tunnel.
///
/// Behind this interface sit two more components and a host import,
/// each doing the one thing its runtime is good at. This Rust
/// component (`wosh-client`) owns everything long-lived: it parses the
/// connection string, dials over `polymorph:iroh`, pumps bytes between
/// the wire and the SSH stack, and relays signatures and prompt
/// batches. The SSH protocol itself lives in `wosh:ssh-core` -- a
/// sans-I/O Go component (`x/crypto/ssh`) that only ever sees fed
/// bytes and tick calls. Keys live behind the host's `identity-store`;
/// no private-key handle exists anywhere in the component graph.
///
/// Every function here is `async` because the work behind it genuinely
/// suspends (the iroh dial, a signature from the store); wit-bindgen
/// tracks this component's spawned tasks, so -- unlike the previous
/// single-Go-component client -- no caller-supplied keepalive is
/// needed, and there is none.
interface terminal {
  /// Where the session currently stands.
  variant status {
    /// Dialing over iroh, or the SSH transport handshake is running.
    connecting,
    /// Key exchange finished: `host-key-fingerprint` is readable and
    /// the session is parked here until `confirm-host-key` resolves
    /// it. Nothing has been sent to the server yet -- not a password,
    /// not even a public-key probe.
    host-key-check,
    /// The host key was accepted; authentication is in flight.
    authenticating,
    /// Keyboard-interactive auth: the server issued a prompt batch and
    /// authentication is parked until `answer-prompts` supplies the
    /// answers. Read the batch with `pending-prompts`.
    auth-prompts,
    /// Pty and shell are up; `write-input`/`drain-output` carry the
    /// interactive session.
    ready,
    /// Terminal state: the dial, handshake, auth, or shell failed, or
    /// the session ended. The string is a human-readable reason.
    closed(string),
  }

  /// Where the TRANSPORT stands, which is a different question from
  /// `status` and deliberately orthogonal to it.
  ///
  /// A resume in flight leaves `status` at whatever the core reports
  /// -- usually `ready` -- because the SSH session IS alive: it is
  /// mid-session and merely not receiving bytes, and claiming `closed`
  /// for a session about to continue would be a lie the page could not
  /// take back. This variant is where the page learns the transport's
  /// truth, so it can say "reconnecting" over a session it must not
  /// declare dead.
  enum link-state {
    /// A connection is installed and believed live.
    attached,
    /// The transport died and the resume machine is dialing right now.
    reconnecting,
    /// The transport died while the page was suspended. Nothing will
    /// be attempted until `wake`.
    stalled,
  }

  /// Why a session ended -- the machine-readable half of `closed`.
  ///
  /// This classification exists precisely so a page can decide about
  /// automatic reconnection WITHOUT parsing the human-readable reason
  /// string that `status` carries.
  enum close-kind {
    /// The SSH session concluded at the protocol level: the shell
    /// exited, the server disconnected, or the page detached.
    /// Reconnecting would start a genuinely new session.
    ended,
    /// The session never reached `ready`: the dial, the tunnel
    /// handshake, the host-key gate or authentication failed. Retrying
    /// without changing something is unlikely to differ.
    failed,
    /// An established session was lost mid-flight and could not be
    /// resumed -- resume refused, replay gap or overflow, resume
    /// budget exhausted, or a transport death on the legacy v1 pipe
    /// that has no resume at all. The one kind where automatically
    /// opening a fresh session on the same parameters is a reasonable
    /// reaction.
    lost,
  }

  /// One keyboard-interactive prompt: the text to display, and whether
  /// the user's answer should be echoed as typed (`false` means mask
  /// it, password-style).
  record prompt {
    text: string,
    echo: bool,
  }

  /// One server-issued batch of keyboard-interactive prompts (RFC
  /// 4256). The server drives: it may send any number of batches in
  /// sequence, each with any number of prompts, before deciding.
  record prompt-batch {
    /// Free-text preamble to display above the prompts. Often empty;
    /// PAM setups use it for OTP guidance and expiry warnings.
    instruction: string,
    prompts: list<prompt>,
  }

  /// This browser's SSH identity, as an OpenSSH `authorized_keys` line
  /// (`ssh-ed25519 AAAA... wosh-browser`).
  ///
  /// The key pair lives behind the host's `identity-store`, so it
  /// outlives this instance wherever the host has durable storage: in
  /// a browser the same line keeps working across page reloads. The
  /// private half is a NON-EXTRACTABLE WebCrypto key, and no handle to
  /// it exists in this component: signing during SSH publickey auth
  /// asks the store, and nothing -- not this component, not the page,
  /// not an XSS payload -- can read the key out. Fetched once per
  /// instance and cached.
  identity-openssh: async func() -> result<string, string>;

  /// This client's PASSKEY identity as an OpenSSH `authorized_keys`
  /// line (`sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com AAAA... wosh-passkey`),
  /// or `none` when no passkey is enrolled.
  ///
  /// The line is an ordinary security-key entry -- the same kind a
  /// hardware token gets -- and installing it is the whole procedure
  /// on the target. (Servers older than OpenSSH 10.3 must also enable
  /// the algorithm; see `passkey-store`.) Its `application` field is
  /// the site's
  /// domain rather than the customary `ssh:`, because that is what the
  /// authenticator actually signs over -- see `passkey-store`.
  ///
  /// It is also the portable form of the identity: pasting this line
  /// into another device that can reach the same passkey re-creates
  /// the identity there (`adopt-passkey`).
  passkey-openssh: async func() -> result<option<string>, string>;

  /// Enrol a passkey as an SSH identity, replacing any previous one,
  /// and return its `authorized_keys` line. Runs a registration
  /// ceremony: the user will be asked to approve it.
  enroll-passkey: async func() -> result<string, string>;

  /// Recover this client's passkey identity from the credential itself,
  /// and return its `authorized_keys` line.
  ///
  /// For when the passkey outlived the browser storage that described
  /// it -- evicted, cleared, or a different browser profile. It needs
  /// nothing external: not the line, not the target, not another
  /// device. It asks the authenticator to sign twice (two touches, and
  /// the SAME passkey must be chosen both times), and works the public
  /// half out of the two signatures.
  ///
  /// Prefer `adopt-passkey` where the line IS to hand: one touch
  /// instead of two, and no chance of picking the wrong credential
  /// halfway through.
  recover-passkey: async func() -> result<string, string>;

  /// Adopt a passkey identity enrolled on another device from its
  /// `authorized_keys` line (as `passkey-openssh` printed it there),
  /// and return the line as this client parsed it back. The passkey
  /// itself must be reachable from this device; only its public half
  /// travels in the line.
  adopt-passkey: async func(line: string) -> result<string, string>;

  /// Stop offering the enrolled passkey. The credential itself is
  /// untouched -- it lives in the authenticator, not here.
  forget-passkey: async func() -> result<_, string>;

  resource session {
    /// Parse `connstring` (as printed and QR-encoded by the listener),
    /// dial that peer over iroh, open the tunnel stream, present the
    /// pairing token when the connstring carries one, and start the
    /// SSH transport handshake. `cols`/`rows` size the pty requested
    /// once authenticated. Resolves once the dial either succeeded
    /// (poll `status` from here) or failed outright.
    connect: static async func(connstring: string, user: string, cols: u16, rows: u16) -> result<session, string>;

    status: async func() -> status;

    /// Where the transport stands, independently of `status`: whether
    /// a connection is installed, a resume is dialing, or the session
    /// is parked waiting for `wake`.
    link-state: async func() -> link-state;

    /// Why the session ended, machine-readably; `none` while it is
    /// still alive.
    close-kind: async func() -> option<close-kind>;

    /// The server's host key fingerprint, in the form `ssh-keygen -lf`
    /// prints (`SHA256:...`), so it can be compared by eye against
    /// what the listener's operator published.
    host-key-fingerprint: async func() -> option<string>;

    /// The user's verdict on that fingerprint. Rejecting tears the
    /// connection down having sent no credentials at all. Accepting
    /// merely records consent -- authentication begins when one of the
    /// `authenticate-*` calls supplies a credential.
    confirm-host-key: async func(accept: bool);

    /// Offer a password. Fails immediately if the host key has not been
    /// confirmed. Otherwise it latches the credential and RESOLVES AT
    /// ONCE -- authentication runs in the background, so poll `status`
    /// until `ready` or `closed`. (These calls cannot wait for the
    /// outcome: an async export that blocks on an in-language primitive
    /// can have its task declared complete out from under it.)
    authenticate-password: async func(password: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Authenticate with this browser's non-extractable WebCrypto key
    /// (see `identity-openssh`, whose line must be installed in the
    /// target account's `authorized_keys`). The signature is produced
    /// by the authenticator; the private key is never in this
    /// component's memory. Same host-key gating, and the same
    /// resolve-immediately-then-poll contract as
    /// `authenticate-password`.
    authenticate-publickey: async func() -> result<_, string>;

    /// Authenticate with the enrolled passkey (see `passkey-openssh`,
    /// whose line must be installed in the target account's
    /// `authorized_keys`). Fails immediately if no passkey is
    /// enrolled.
    ///
    /// Unlike every other method here, this one needs the human DURING
    /// authentication, not before it: the server's demand for a
    /// signature is what triggers the authenticator ceremony, and that
    /// cannot be known until the session id exists. Callers driving a
    /// browser should expect the ceremony to want a user gesture and
    /// should not treat the pause as a stall. Same host-key gating and
    /// the same resolve-immediately-then-poll contract as the others.
    authenticate-passkey: async func() -> result<_, string>;

    /// Start keyboard-interactive authentication (RFC 4256): the
    /// server drives the exchange through prompt batches, which is how
    /// PAM OTP/2FA setups -- and sshds with plain password auth
    /// disabled -- steer clients. Same host-key gating, and the same
    /// resolve-immediately-then-poll contract as the other
    /// `authenticate-*` calls. Each time `status` reports
    /// `auth-prompts`, read `pending-prompts`, collect answers, and
    /// call `answer-prompts`; batches repeat until the server decides.
    /// Zero-prompt batches are answered internally without surfacing.
    authenticate-interactive: async func() -> result<_, string>;

    /// Let the server steer method selection, the way a stock ssh
    /// client does. Every supported method is on offer -- publickey
    /// first (the enrolled passkey, then this browser's key; both when
    /// both exist), then password, then keyboard-interactive -- and
    /// the client walks that order constrained to the methods the
    /// server says can continue.
    ///
    /// Offering both keys is cheap and is how a passkey degrades
    /// gracefully: each key is offered unsigned first, so an sshd too
    /// old for webauthn, or configured not to accept it, simply
    /// declines the passkey and the browser key answers instead --
    /// within the same connection, with no ceremony spent. A key the
    /// server accepts is the only one ever signed for, so at most one
    /// authenticator ceremony happens.
    ///
    /// The browser key needs no human input, so when it is installed
    /// the session reaches `ready` without a single prompt; a passkey
    /// the server accepts will ask for the human. Whatever needs input
    /// -- the password if the server steers there, an OTP round --
    /// surfaces through the same machinery as
    /// `authenticate-interactive`: `status` reports `auth-prompts`,
    /// read `pending-prompts`, `answer-prompts`, repeat until the
    /// server decides. (A password request is a one-prompt masked
    /// batch.) Same host-key gating, and the same
    /// resolve-immediately-then-poll contract as the other
    /// `authenticate-*` calls.
    authenticate-auto: async func() -> result<_, string>;

    /// The prompt batch authentication is parked on, present while
    /// `status` is `auth-prompts`.
    pending-prompts: async func() -> option<prompt-batch>;

    /// Answer the pending batch: one string per prompt, in order.
    /// Latches the answers and RESOLVES AT ONCE (the same no-blocking
    /// rule as every export here); the exchange continues in the
    /// background, so poll `status` for what the server did next.
    /// Fails if no batch is pending or the count does not match.
    answer-prompts: async func(answers: list<string>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Keystrokes or pasted bytes from the terminal (xterm.js
    /// `onData`). No local echo or line editing happens here -- the
    /// remote pty owns both, exactly like a real terminal.
    write-input: async func(data: list<u8>);

    /// Propagate a terminal resize (xterm.js `onResize`).
    resize: async func(cols: u16, rows: u16);

    /// Pty output accumulated since the last call: raw bytes, in
    /// order, to be written straight to xterm.js. Returns promptly
    /// with whatever is buffered, possibly empty.
    drain-output: async func() -> list<u8>;

    /// True once the remote shell exited or the connection died.
    exited: async func() -> bool;

    /// The shell's exit status, once known.
    exit-status: async func() -> option<s32>;

    /// Stop the session and close the iroh connection.
    detach: async func();

    /// The page is going away: backgrounded, frozen, or about to be
    /// discarded. Stand down -- do not spend the resume budget on a
    /// device whose network is being taken away, and do not wake its
    /// radio to try. Nothing is torn down: the session keeps its core,
    /// its endpoint and its place in the listener's parked registry,
    /// and a transport that dies while suspended leaves the session
    /// stalled rather than dead.
    ///
    /// Idempotent, and safe to call when there is nothing to suspend.
    suspend: async func();

    /// The page is back. Resume now if the transport went while we
    /// were away, rather than waiting out a backoff that was measured
    /// for a page that could see it. Idempotent; a no-op on a session
    /// that is still connected, and on one that is genuinely over.
    wake: async func();
  }
}
